{"candidates":[{"content":"Make attack edges first-class warrant-carrying nodes: for each attack A→B add an edge-node E with E→B, and allow any argument C to attack E (C→E). Compute the grounded extension on the expanded AF. An attack only refutes B when E is accepted (every attacker of E is out), so attacking the warrant itself defeats the refutation. This preserves determinism and polynomial cost because the expansion adds one node and at most one extra edge per existing attack and the grounded fixpoint algorithm is unchanged. Committed roots remain valid if no accepted attack-edge points at them. Weakness: accepted A and accepted E both attack B, which can double-count; a merge/identity rule is needed to decide whether the original A→B still exists when E is out.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0378275f89478d38","quote":"Carriage is a relation, not a field."},{"block":"1dd041117ca8c54a","quote":"Nothing in this system is refuted by being disliked."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"Accrual as collective defeat via collector nodes: for a bounded set of weak attacks {A_i→B} that should defeat B only together, add a collector node C, add edges from every attacker of each A_i to C, and add C→B; replace the individual weak attacks with the single collective attack. In the grounded extension C is accepted exactly when every A_i is accepted, so B is out only under joint accrual. The construction is linear in the sum of attacker sets and the fixpoint remains polynomial. Criticizable: the encoding assumes attacks on the A_i are independent and may create unintended defense paths through C; overlapping accrual sets can blow up unless bounded.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0c302d11d621778b","quote":"The grounded extension itself was never the problem: given the edges, the label computation is already a pure deterministic fixpoint."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.2},{"content":"Priority-filtered attack relation: assign each argument a finite priority level from a configured total preorder, and retain an attack A→B only when priority(A) is strictly greater than priority(B), with committed roots given a unique top priority. This is a pure graph transformation before the grounded fixpoint, so determinism and polynomial cost are unchanged; reinstatement remains derived because the filtered graph is still an AF. Root validity is preserved because no retained attack targets a top-priority root. Weakness: priority is exogenous and cannot itself be challenged; if priorities are learned or contested, the semantics silently depends on an unadjudicated input.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0e806619395ec8f4","quote":"Labels are a pure function of the graph."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.55},{"content":"Add bounded defense-depth labels alongside grounded status: run the grounded fixpoint once, then compute for each accepted node the minimal length of a defense tree (or ∞ if none) and for each out node the set of attacking accepted nodes. These depth tags are derived from the final fixed point, not part of the iteration, so determinism and polynomial cost are preserved. They strengthen the semantics by exposing why a conjecture is accepted or refuted without changing any label. Committed roots get depth 0 and remain valid. Weakness: post-hoc tags cannot influence the fixpoint, so they are explanatory only; if the goal is to change acceptance, this candidate is too weak.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0e806619395ec8f4","quote":"Labels are a pure function of the graph."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.65},{"content":"Two-level warrant validity: split attack edges into provisional and committed based on an inner grounded labelling of a warrant graph. First compute grounded extension on the subgraph of warrants only; then construct the outer attack relation using only attacks whose warrant node is accepted; finally compute the grounded extension on that derived graph. This gives a compositional, polynomial two-pass semantics and preserves root validity because roots are untouched by warrant filtering. Weakness: the inner and outer labellings are not mutually recursive; an attack on a warrant by an argument that is only accepted in the outer labelling is invisible to the inner pass, so some self-referential challenges to warrants are missed.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0378275f89478d38","quote":"Carriage is a relation, not a field."},{"block":"1dd041117ca8c54a","quote":"Nothing in this system is refuted by being disliked."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"Add support edges as defender edges: for each support relation B supports A and each attacker C of A, add an edge B→C. This lets an accepted supporter B defeat A's attackers, strengthening A within the same grounded fixpoint. The graph remains an AF, so determinism, polynomial cost, and derived reinstatement are preserved; committed roots are untouched by the added edges. Weakness: support becomes a universal counterattack and cannot express degrees of support; a single accepted supporter can overturn many independent attacks, and the semantics depends on choosing which supports are active before the fixpoint.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"0c302d11d621778b","quote":"The grounded extension itself was never the problem: given the edges, the label computation is already a pure deterministic fixpoint."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"src-8ddb400f3c2d52a681266c7cebc3b1898bac6750"}],"typicality":0.15}],"simulation_proposals":[]}